.TH ATF 1 "September 2026" "atf 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
atf \- run a command at a specified time, in the foreground
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B atf
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fITIME\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR]...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B atf
waits until a loosely specified time, then runs
.IR COMMAND ,
or exits like
.BR sleep (1)
when no command is given.
It stays in the foreground, so it can be chained in scripts:
.PP
.RS 4
atf 23:00 && echo done
.RE
.PP
Times without an explicit offset are interpreted in the
.B TZ
environment's time zone. Explicit offsets in
.I TIME
win over
.BR TZ .
.PP
The wait uses an absolute realtime clock, so NTP steps and
suspend/resume on laptops behave correctly: the command runs at the
requested wall-clock time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Do not print the status line.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-print
Print the target Unix time and exit without waiting.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
If
.I TIME
is in the past, run immediately instead of refusing.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-pretty ", " \-\-progress
Draw a live one-line countdown with a progress bar and time remaining.
Falls back to the plain status line when standard error is not a
terminal. Suppressed by
.BR \-q .
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-every " \fIN\fR"
Run
.I COMMAND
every
.IR N ,
forever: wait, run, wait, run. The first run is at
.IR TIME ,
or immediately when
.I TIME
is omitted. The loop stops only on a signal; a failing command does not
stop it. Requires a
.IR COMMAND .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SH TIME FORMAT
.I TIME
is parsed with the same grammar as GNU
.BR date (1)
and
.BR at (1)
(gnulib's parse-datetime). Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
23:00, 4pm, 0200, midnight   clock time; rolls to tomorrow if past
:55, :55:30                 next time the clock reaches that mark
2026-10-26 23:00+07:00      explicit UTC offset
2026-10-26T23:00:00Z        ISO 8601
tomorrow 23:00, next monday relative words
+2 hours, 2 days ago        relative to now
90s, 2.5s, 2h30m, 1d, 250ms duration from now
@1800000000                 seconds since the Unix epoch
now                         run immediately
.fi
.RE
.PP
A clock time with no date part that is already past means the next
day. An explicitly dated time in the past is an error unless
.B \-f
is given.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TZ
Time zone used for times without an explicit offset, for example:
.PP
.RS 4
TZ=Asia/Bangkok atf 23:00 -- cmd
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status is that of
.IR COMMAND ,
or 0 when there is no command. With
.BR \-e ,
the loop only exits on a signal, reporting 128 plus the signal number.
Otherwise:
.TP
.B 1
An explicitly dated
.I TIME
is in the past and
.B \-f
was not given.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or time-parse error, or a failed system call.
.TP
.B 126
.I COMMAND
was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
.I COMMAND
was not found.
.TP
.B 128+\fIN\fR
Terminated by signal
.IR N .
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a backup later tonight, and only chain when it starts:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
atf 23:00 -- tar \-czf /backup/home.tgz /home
atf tomorrow 06:30 -- ./deploy.sh
atf +2 hours                # just wait, like sleep 7200
atf :30 -- ./poll.sh        # every hour at :30
atf \-e 5m -- ./health.sh   # every 5 minutes from now
atf \-P '2027-01-01 00:00'  # pretty countdown to the new year
.fi
.RE
.PP
Shell syntax needs an explicit shell:
.PP
.RS 4
atf \+1h -- sh \-c 'make && ./deploy.sh'
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR at (1),
.BR date (1),
.BR sleep (1),
.BR watch (1),
.BR timeout (1),
.BR cron (8)
